              {
                "id": "mem_366d8893447f472e9f9e3892e94558a8",
                "kind": "episode",
                "text": "To diagnose a pod that is in `CrashLoopBackOff`, you can start by running the following `kubectl` commands:\n\n1. **Check Pod Status:**\n   ```sh\n   kubectl get pods -n my-ns | grep my-pod\n   ```\n   This command will show the status of your pod and confirm if it is indeed in `CrashLoopBackOff`.\n\n2. **Describe the Pod:**\n   ```sh\n   kubectl describe pod my-pod -n my-ns\n   ```\n   The `describe` command provides detailed information about the pod, including events that led to its current state.\n\n3. **Check Container Logs:**\n   ```sh\n   kubectl logs my-pod -n my-ns\n   ```\n   Examining the container logs can help identify the cause of the crashes or errors that are leading to the `CrashLoopBackOff` state.\n\nThese commands will give you a good starting point for diagnosing and resolving the issue with your pod.",
                "sources": [
                  "run://run-46beaab2042e/answer"
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "mem_13f1640011e449a5b0dfa5c83d6f2f5b",
                "kind": "episode",
                "text": "To diagnose a pod that is stuck in `ImagePullBackOff`, you can start by running the following `kubectl` commands:\n\n1. **Check Pod Status:**\n   ```sh\n   kubectl get pods -n my-ns | grep my-pod\n   ```\n2. **Describe the Pod:**\n   ```sh\n   kubectl describe pod my-pod -n my-ns\n   ```\n3. **Check Container Logs:**\n   ```sh\n   kubectl logs my-pod -n my-ns\n   ```\n\nThese commands will provide insights into why the pod is in `ImagePullBackOff` and help you identify the underlying issue.",
                "sources": [
                  "run://run-91c9c62008a4/answer"
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "mem_47da781afa084c36b27da49a0f105123",
